1
00:00:00,840 --> 00:00:08,200
Hey there, Spanish student! Would you know
how to survive in a Spanish hotel? Booking,

2
00:00:08,200 --> 00:00:15,000
checking in, asking for a pillow,
complaining if something doesn't work? Today

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:21,360
we're going to find out. I'm Mercedes and
this is Spanish Lessons with Mercedes,

4
00:00:21,360 --> 00:00:26,200
the Spanish channel open to the world.
So how are we going to do this?

5
00:00:26,760 --> 00:00:35,840
Well, we're going to simulate a real situation:
a hotel in Spain. To make it easier, I'm going to

6
00:00:35,840 --> 00:00:45,400
play both roles — the guest and the receptionist.
I'll read the dialogues slowly so

7
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:53,200
you can follow along and notice the details.
You'll see it in two different colors on screen.

8
00:00:53,200 --> 00:01:01,320
Red for the receptionist and green for the
guest. That way you can tell

9
00:01:01,320 --> 00:01:09,960
who's speaking at each moment. And here's the key.
Listen carefully: first, pause the video as many times

10
00:01:09,960 --> 00:01:20,880
as you need, and then repeat out loud. Copy my
tone, my pauses, my rhythm — and there's no

11
00:01:20,880 --> 00:01:32,040
need to memorize the dialogue, just live it
as if you were really there. Ready? Let's begin.

12
00:01:34,400 --> 00:01:42,920
Let's go with scene one: how to make a
reservation by phone. Imagine the following.

13
00:01:42,920 --> 00:01:51,840
You want to spend the weekend at a hotel.
You pick up the phone and call to book. Listen

14
00:01:51,840 --> 00:01:59,640
first, then it's your turn. — Good afternoon,
Hotel Mediterráneo. How can I help you?

15
00:02:00,280 --> 00:02:10,400
— Good afternoon. I'd like to book a room
for this weekend. — For how many nights?

16
00:02:10,400 --> 00:02:20,720
— Two nights. — Would you like a single or a
double? — Double, please. — Would you like

17
00:02:20,720 --> 00:02:31,440
breakfast included? — Yes, please. — Who should
I put the reservation under? — Mercedes. — Thank you.

18
00:02:31,440 --> 00:02:37,960
Tell me, did you follow the conversation well?
Did you understand it all? And before we go on,

19
00:02:37,960 --> 00:02:47,000
notice something you just heard. I said
"me gustaría reservar" — I would like to book. In these

20
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:56,480
formal situations we don't say "quiero una
habitación" — I want a room. It's more polite to say

21
00:02:57,360 --> 00:03:03,840
"me gustaría reservar una habitación."
We're using the conditional of the verb "gustar."

22
00:03:03,840 --> 00:03:13,560
You can also say "quisiera reservar una
habitación." Here we're using the imperfect

23
00:03:13,560 --> 00:03:25,800
subjunctive of the verb "querer." Both are polite
formulas — the conditional and the imperfect subjunctive.

24
00:03:25,800 --> 00:03:33,640
Let's move on to scene two. Imagine you already
have a reservation, but in the end you can't travel.

25
00:03:33,640 --> 00:03:41,320
You call the hotel to cancel it. Let's listen
to the dialogue. — Good afternoon, Hotel Mediterráneo.

26
00:03:41,320 --> 00:03:48,680
How can I help you? — Good afternoon. I have a
reservation under the name Mercedes, and I need

27
00:03:48,680 --> 00:03:57,320
to cancel it, please. — I'm sorry to hear that.
Could you give me the confirmation number? — Yes, one moment,

28
00:03:57,320 --> 00:04:07,720
please. — Perfect, I've cancelled it. Would you
like to make a new reservation later on? — Not

29
00:04:07,720 --> 00:04:16,560
right now, thank you. Maybe next time. — Understood.
Have a nice day. — You too, thank you very much.

30
00:04:16,560 --> 00:04:24,680
Now, before we move on, write down these two
verbs: "anular" and "cancelar." They mean the same —

31
00:04:24,680 --> 00:04:32,640
to decide that a reservation or service that was
planned will no longer take place. You can use either:

32
00:04:32,640 --> 00:04:42,880
"anular la reserva" or "cancelar la reserva."
And notice something else: when we say "anularla"

33
00:04:42,880 --> 00:04:52,520
or "cancelarla," the pronoun "la" attaches to
the infinitive because it replaces "reserva."

34
00:04:54,240 --> 00:05:02,360
Let's move on to scene three. Now we're at the
hotel reception, and we're going to check in.

35
00:05:02,360 --> 00:05:11,920
Imagine this: you've arrived at the hotel,
suitcase in hand, and you head to reception.

36
00:05:11,920 --> 00:05:20,000
Time to check in. Listen to the dialogue.
— Good afternoon. Welcome to Hotel Mediterráneo.

37
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:29,680
— I have a reservation under the name Mercedes.
— Could you show me your passport, please?

38
00:05:29,680 --> 00:05:39,480
— Yes, of course, here you go. — Your room is
305. Here's your key card. Breakfast is served

39
00:05:39,480 --> 00:05:50,960
from 7 to 10 in the morning on the 1st floor.
— Excuse me, a question — is there wifi here?

40
00:05:50,960 --> 00:06:00,320
— Yes, of course. The network is called Hotel
Mediterráneo. The password is on the welcome card.

41
00:06:00,320 --> 00:06:09,400
— Perfect, thank you. — Enjoy your stay. And
before we continue, another pause: did you notice

42
00:06:09,400 --> 00:06:22,360
I said "in 305" and not "in room 305"? In a hotel
it's already understood that we're talking about the room.

43
00:06:22,360 --> 00:06:28,320
Let's move on to the next scene. Scene four:
problems in the room.

44
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:34,280
Imagine this: you've been settled
in your room for a while,

45
00:06:34,280 --> 00:06:44,080
but something isn't working as it should. You go
down to reception to politely complain. Listen

46
00:06:44,080 --> 00:06:53,480
to the dialogue. — Good afternoon. I have a
problem with the air conditioning — it's not working.

47
00:06:53,480 --> 00:07:02,880
— I'm very sorry. We'll send a technician right
away to fix it. — But later — excuse me — the problem

48
00:07:02,880 --> 00:07:11,240
is still there. Would it be possible to change
rooms? — Of course, we have another room available.

49
00:07:14,080 --> 00:07:21,480
Let's go with scene five: asking for an extra
service. You're in your room and you're missing

50
00:07:21,480 --> 00:07:29,840
something. You pick up the phone to ask for it.
Listen: could you bring me one more pillow,

51
00:07:29,840 --> 00:07:39,200
please? — Of course, we'll bring it right away.
And before we continue, another pause so you notice

52
00:07:39,200 --> 00:07:47,360
something. All the questions we've asked so far
have been phrased the same way: "Could you

53
00:07:47,360 --> 00:07:59,200
show me...?", "Could you...?", "Could you all...?"
They all use the same form of address — "usted,"

54
00:07:59,200 --> 00:08:08,560
formal and appropriate in a hotel. But pay close
attention: we use "usted" to address the second

55
00:08:08,560 --> 00:08:22,200
person in formal situations. But watch out —
the verb is conjugated in the third person.

56
00:08:22,200 --> 00:08:29,880
And now, scene six: do you want to extend your
stay at the hotel? Imagine this: you love the hotel,

57
00:08:29,880 --> 00:08:39,280
you don't want to leave yet, and you decide to
ask if you can stay a few more days. Listen

58
00:08:39,280 --> 00:08:49,120
to the dialogue. — Excuse me, a question — would
it be possible to extend my stay one more night?

59
00:08:49,120 --> 00:08:58,600
— Mm, yes, we have availability. Would you like
me to add it to your reservation? — Yes, please.

60
00:09:00,960 --> 00:09:09,520
Imagine this: your stay is over. You go down
to reception to pay and say goodbye.

61
00:09:09,520 --> 00:09:17,160
Listen to the dialogue. — Good morning. We're
checking out of our room. I'd like to pay.

62
00:09:17,160 --> 00:09:24,440
— By card or cash? — By card, please. — We hope
everything was to

63
00:09:24,440 --> 00:09:37,800
your liking, and that you come back soon. It's
been a very pleasant stay. — See you next time.

64
00:09:37,800 --> 00:09:45,560
Wait, don't leave just yet. You've already paid,
but your flight isn't until tonight. Do you still

65
00:09:45,560 --> 00:09:53,000
have some free hours? And you ask the following
at reception: — Excuse me, I've already checked out,

66
00:09:53,000 --> 00:10:04,160
but my flight leaves very late. Could I leave my
luggage here for a few hours? — Of course.

67
00:10:04,160 --> 00:10:11,240
Here's your claim ticket. That's a great help —
enjoy your last few hours in the city. And

68
00:10:11,240 --> 00:10:19,520
before we finish, let's review. You've now seen it
all in this video: booking, cancelling, asking for things,

69
00:10:19,520 --> 00:10:28,000
extending your stay, storing your luggage — always
with the conditional. That's the key to sounding

70
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:36,640
natural in any hotel. See you in the next video.
Take care, Spanish student.
